+
+### Long lists
+
+By default a `ScrollView` builds and lays out every child, on screen or not. For a long list set `lazy` and make the rows the direct children of the `ScrollView`:
+
+```tsx
+<ScrollView lazy padding={8} flex={{ gap: 4 }}>
+  <For each={rows()}>{(row) => <Row row={row} />}</For>
+</ScrollView>
+```
+
+Flutter then builds a row's widgets when it scrolls near the viewport (`ListView.builder`). The rows are laid out along the scroll axis; of `flex` only `gap` applies. The JS side still creates every row, so this removes the Flutter build and layout cost of off-screen rows, not the cost of creating them.
 
